1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:06,206
Here is a sentence about a shuttle, an orbit and a telescope.

2
00:00:06,487 --> 00:00:10,351
A classifier says: Space, one hundred percent confident.

3
00:00:10,631 --> 00:00:12,791
That alone isn't very useful.

4
00:00:13,071 --> 00:00:14,943
What I want to know is why.

5
00:00:15,223 --> 00:00:21,247
So the app highlights every word: green words push towards the answer, and red words push away.

6
00:00:21,527 --> 00:00:27,647
Then I give it a sentence designed to confuse it, about a rocket engine, a car and a hockey puck.

7
00:00:27,927 --> 00:00:31,767
Watch the prediction wobble, and watch which words are voting for what.

8
00:00:32,047 --> 00:00:36,390
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

9
00:00:37,191 --> 00:00:42,591
Deep neural networks are very good at text, but it is hard to say why they gave a particular answer.

10
00:00:42,871 --> 00:00:47,767
This app uses an older technique on purpose, because it can be explained completely.

11
00:00:48,047 --> 00:00:49,919
The pipeline has two parts.

12
00:00:50,199 --> 00:00:59,223
TF-IDF turns a text into numbers: each word gets a score that is high when the word is frequent in this text and rare across the whole collection.

13
00:00:59,503 --> 00:01:03,895
Then logistic regression learns one weight per word for each topic.

14
00:01:04,175 --> 00:01:07,655
Positive weights mean the word is evidence for that topic.

15
00:01:07,935 --> 00:01:10,983
To classify a text, add up the evidence.

16
00:01:11,263 --> 00:01:16,111
Because the model is just a sum, you can read off every word's contribution.

17
00:01:16,911 --> 00:01:24,807
The data is a real dataset called twenty newsgroups: old internet discussion posts, labelled by the group they were posted in.

18
00:01:25,087 --> 00:01:33,343
I picked six groups: space, hockey, graphics, cars, medicine and politics, which here is the gun politics group..

19
00:01:33,623 --> 00:01:39,743
Stop words like the and of are dropped, and words that appear in fewer than three posts are ignored.

20
00:01:40,023 --> 00:01:45,135
The vectoriser learns the vocabulary and the weights on thirty-five hundred training posts.

21
00:01:45,415 --> 00:01:50,671
Then the model predicts the separate test set of twenty-three hundred posts it has never seen.

22
00:01:50,951 --> 00:01:54,167
The app caches all this, so it only trains once.

23
00:01:54,447 --> 00:02:00,207
The accuracy on those unseen posts is shown in the sidebar: eighty-four point eight percent.

24
00:02:01,007 --> 00:02:03,191
This is the explanation itself..

25
00:02:03,471 --> 00:02:06,063
Take the top two topics for this text.

26
00:02:06,343 --> 00:02:15,511
For every word that the model knows, look at the difference between its weight for the winner and for the runner-up, and multiply by the word's TF-IDF value in this text.

27
00:02:15,791 --> 00:02:22,246
A positive number means the word pushed towards the winner, and a negative number means it pushed towards the runner-up.

28
00:02:22,526 --> 00:02:28,334
That number sets the colour and the strength of each highlight, and the eight biggest go in the table.

29
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:31,007
The Space example.

30
00:02:31,286 --> 00:02:37,166
The biggest pushes are orbit, moon, launch, NASA and shuttle, in that order.

31
00:02:37,446 --> 00:02:45,102
The model is a hundred percent sure, and the reason is visible: five strong space words, and nothing pulling the other way.

32
00:02:45,382 --> 00:02:47,255
I click the Hockey example.

33
00:02:47,534 --> 00:02:55,262
Game, playoff, Bruins, win and scored carry it, and the prediction is Hockey with ninety-nine percent confidence.

34
00:02:55,542 --> 00:03:02,598
Now the tricky one: the new rocket engine has more horsepower than any car and could hit a puck faster than a hockey player.

35
00:03:02,878 --> 00:03:08,279
The model says Hockey, but only with fifty-three percent, and Cars has forty-six.

36
00:03:08,559 --> 00:03:10,431
And now we can see why.

37
00:03:10,710 --> 00:03:16,830
The word car pushes towards Cars with a strength of nearly three, and engine pushes the same way.

38
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:22,559
Against that, hockey pushes towards Hockey even harder, and puck and player add to it.

39
00:03:22,839 --> 00:03:25,839
The model isn't confused in a mysterious way.

40
00:03:26,119 --> 00:03:29,311
It is a close vote between two clusters of words.

41
00:03:30,111 --> 00:03:35,799
The second tab shows the highest weighted words for each topic, which the model worked out on its own.

42
00:03:36,079 --> 00:03:43,207
For Space: space, orbit, NASA, moon, launch, earth, spacecraft, shuttle.

43
00:03:43,487 --> 00:03:45,359
Nobody told it that.

44
00:03:45,638 --> 00:03:48,951
These come purely from which words show up in which group.

45
00:03:49,751 --> 00:03:54,599
The confusion matrix shows, for each real topic, what the model predicted.

46
00:03:54,878 --> 00:03:57,566
Bright diagonal cells are correct answers.

47
00:03:57,846 --> 00:04:05,790
The app's own caption says mistakes are mostly between related topics like Space and Medicine, but in my run the biggest error cells are different.

48
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:16,198
The largest are Medicine posts labelled Cars, forty of them, then Space posts labelled Cars, thirty-eight, and Politics posts labelled Cars, thirty-two.

49
00:04:16,478 --> 00:04:20,366
Why Cars absorbs so many mistakes, I haven't investigated.

50
00:04:21,166 --> 00:04:24,646
Being able to see the reasons changes how you trust a model.

51
00:04:24,926 --> 00:04:28,550
For the easy examples, the right words carry the decision.

52
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:31,470
For the tricky one, you can see the argument.

53
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:38,158
And when I looked at the errors, I found something I wouldn't have guessed: most of the biggest mistakes land on Cars.

54
00:04:38,958 --> 00:04:43,206
Eighty-four point eight percent on six topics is decent, not great.

55
00:04:43,486 --> 00:04:49,630
The data is old internet posts from the nineties, so the model knows that language, not modern text.

56
00:04:49,910 --> 00:05:02,054
The word contributions explain this particular model, which is a simple linear one; they don't explain a neural network, and they ignore word order and negation, so not good would count the same as good.

57
00:05:02,334 --> 00:05:06,462
The colours compare only the top two topics, not all six.

58
00:05:06,742 --> 00:05:11,806
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

59
00:05:12,606 --> 00:05:21,294
Next I'd let you train on your own labelled text, add a negation test, and put the same sentence through a neural model so you can compare the two explanations.

60
00:05:21,574 --> 00:05:26,062
The code is in the description, with no API key and no cost.

61
00:05:26,342 --> 00:05:33,566
If you enjoyed seeing inside the model, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me which sentence should try to fool it next.
